  "query_preview": "..Today...Ahead of an approaching trough, deep monsoonal\nmoisture will be advected northward along the Colorado River Valley.\n06Z RAP guidance forecasts around 1.00-1.20 inches of PWAT across\nmuch of western Arizona, with around 0.80-1.00 inches elsewhere. \nWhile 15-25 kt storm motions may limit the overall residency time \nof a single storm, the potential for training storms will largely \nnegate this. Therefore, flash flooding will be the primary threat \ntoday. The greatest risk looks to be largely across northwest \nArizona, where coverage of storms (and potential for training) \nwill be the greatest. HREF PMM for >1\" of rain in 3 hours largely \npin point this area as well. A Flash Flood Watch is in effect for \nportions of northwestern Arizona through this evening for this \nthreat.\n\nIn addition to the flash flood threat, a few strong to severe storms \ncan not be totally given 0-6 km shear of around 25-35 kts per the \n06Z RAP. Instability may be limited by cloud coverage this morning, \nhowever any areas that remain fairly clear through the morning and \nearly afternoon will be at the greatest risk for stronger storms. \nGusty outflow winds and large hail will be the main hazards from any \nsevere storms.\n\nOtherwise, much cooler temperatures are forecast today given the \nincrease in cloud cover. Afternoon highs look to be around 5-10F \nbelow normal for this time of year.\n\nSaturday and Sunday...The aforementioned trough lingers across the \nwest on Saturday, before lifting by Sunday. The center of the high \npressure remains pushed to the east, over the Ark-La-Tex region. \n\nCoverage of showers and thunderstorms begins to tick downwards \nthrough the weekend. For Saturday, the isolated to scattered storms \nlook possible from the Mogollon Rim and northward. By Sunday, the \nfocus looks to shift further east, with the best chances across the \nhigher terrain of eastern Arizona. Flash flooding will remain the \nmain concern through the weekend, but a couple stronger storms will \nbe possible given the influence of the trough still. Outside of \nstorms, slightly warmer, but still cool temperatures remain over \nnorthern Arizona with less and less cloud cover expected each day.\n \nMonday through Thursday...High pressure slowly begins to retrograde \nand strengthen over the southwest through the coming week. Warming \ntemperatures ensue across northern Arizona as a result, with \nafternoon highs returning to near-normal to slightly (~5F) above \nnormal.\n\nWith monsoonal moisture still in place, precipitation chances look \nto still continue into the coming week. However, as the ridge \nstrengthens, afternoon convection will become suppressed. Therefore, \nmuch of the activity looks to be isolated in nature, and largely \nfocused on the higher terrain.",
